26-09-08 Release 4.3 spacebub

	* Added:
		- A Replay panel on the profile page, above Multiplayer. A profile
		  either records what is played or plays a recording back, and the
		  panel folds away when it does neither
		- Demos are kept in a "replays" folder inside the profile's own folder,
		  beside its port config and its saves, whether or not the port was
		  given a config of its own. What is in there is listed for playback,
		  newest first, and a demo from anywhere else can be browsed for
		- Source ports ZDL4 lists have first class support and dynamically
		  get their command line arguments adjusted. For user imported ports
		  the launch arguments can be adjusted from the profiles page
		- MBF and Doom Legacy record and play back inside DOSBox: the replays
		  folder is mounted as a drive of its own. A name DOS cannot spell is
		  said before the launch rather than by it, as is a demo that has been
		  deleted since it was picked and a recording about to write over one
		- Supported source ports get a new Saves section in the profiles where
		  a save game can be directly launched
		- {savefile} stands for the save the profile is set to load. A
		  profile writing its own command line is handed it along with the
		  rest
		- {replaydir} stands for the profile's replays folder. A profile
		  writing its own command line is handed it along with the rest
		- PrBoom+ and Boom are now part of the ZDL4 engine roster with first
		  class support
		- Better support for custom images. Profiles now show custom packs'
		  images as a thumbnail when possible, when not falls back to the
		  selected base game's art
	* Changed:
		- The engines page only refreshes the source ports on application start
		  instead of every time the page is opened since GitHub is rate limited
		  per hour. Check again is still there to force it.
	* Fixed:
		- Badges ran off the edge of a library card when a profile had more to
		  say than the card had room for. What fits is drawn, and the rest is
		  a count on the end with the words a hover away
		- A source port fetched for the first time did not report completion
		  and got stuck at 100%.
		- Check again did not update already installed ports
		- Was not possible to add a directory as an add-on which some ports'
		  mods rely on

2026-09-08 Release 4.2 spacebub

	* Changed:
		- The interface has been rewritten in Slint, which is compiled into the
		  binary: ZDL4 is one file with nothing to carry beside it, and no longer
		  needs Qt
		- There is no "Default" profile any more. A fresh config carries no
		  profiles at all, the last one can be deleted like any other, and
		  nothing outside the profile list needs one to be there: a game on the
		  library shelf still launches on its own
	* Added:
		- {extracfg} stands for the second config a vanilla port keeps, which
		  is the one -extraconfig names. A profile writing its own command
		  line is handed it along with the rest
		- The words a command can be written with are listed under the field,
		  each one hovered for what it stands for
	* Fixed:
	    - Use after free bug in the config loading path
	    - A value longer than the field it is in scrolls with the cursor
	      instead of being selected past the edge of the box
	    - A source port ZDL had fetched no longer has to be fetched again to be
	      used. Whatever is unpacked in the data directory is put back into the
	      list even for a fresh config. It is still marked as one ZDL fetched,
	      and removing it still deletes what was unpacked

2026-09-06 Release 4.1 spacebub

	* Added:
		- An Engines page, holding the source ports this config knows about and
		  a list of the ones ZDL can fetch. A port is downloaded, unpacked into
		  the data directory and added to the list in one press, and updated the
		  same way when the project releases something newer
		- What is fetched is kept in a "downloads" folder in the data directory
		  and used again rather than downloaded twice. Settings says how much is
		  being kept and empties it
		- MBF and Doom Legacy are among the ports that can be fetched, and are
		  set up as DOS programs, so they are launched inside DOSBox
		- Zandronum can be fetched too, on Windows: the project's own build
		  rather than a GitHub release
		- A profile can write its own command line, with {source_port}, {game},
		  {addon_1} upwards, {profile}, {cfgdir} and {savedir} standing for what
		  the rest of its page would have worked out. A word the profile cannot
		  fill in is said under the field and stops the launch
		- A profile on a DOS port gives DOSBox the whole screen, which is a
		  switch on the profile's own page
		- The cards on the Library and Engines pages are put in whatever order
		  suits, by dragging one onto the place another is in
	* Changed:
		- Per profile port configs and their saves have moved into a "profiles"
		  folder inside the data directory. Configs written by an earlier ZDL
		  are not found there and have to be moved by hand: everything under
		  ~/.local/share/qzdl (%APPDATA%\qZDL on Windows) that is a profile's
		  folder goes into ~/.local/share/qzdl/profiles
		- Source ports are set up on the Engines page instead of in Settings,
		  which now holds only what launching does and where the config is kept
		- Ports older than Boom are no longer handed -iwad, which they have
		  never heard of and which made them fail to start. They are pointed at
		  the game with $DOOMWADDIR instead, inside DOSBox as well as outside
		- A port is handed only the switches it has. A BEX patch goes on as -deh
		  where -bex is unknown and as -dehacked on Doom Legacy, Helion is not
		  handed -respawn, and +map, +exec and a netgame are the ZDoom family's
		- Both kinds of DeHackEd patch now go behind one switch rather than one
		  each, since a port reads the first it is given and stops looking
    * Fixed:
        - On windows, window snapping now works.
        - A DOS launch no longer loses what DOS cannot name. Anything longer
          than {8}.{3} (aaaaaaaa.bbb), and the game itself where the port has
          no -iwad, is copied into a .doswaddir folder in the data directory
          under a name that works, and that folder is mounted with the rest.
          A port that keeps wads of its own (Doom Legacy's doom3.wad) has them
          carried along, since it looks for those where it was told the game is
        - Reduced the memory consumption of the launcher by quite a bit (still
          high because QML :/)

2026-09-04 Release 4.0 spacebub

	* Changed:
		- Configuration is now stored as JSON (zdl.json) instead of INI
		- The user config now lives in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qzdl (~/.config/qzdl)
		  instead of the old "Vectec Software" directory. On Windows it stays
		  in AppData
		- A config from an older ZDL is still read from its previous location,
		  migrated automatically on first run and left untouched. .zdl files
		  keep their original INI format
		- Each profile keeps its own file list and always keeps it, so the
		  option to forget the file list on exit is gone
		- The config is written a moment after anything changes it rather than
		  only on the way out, and opening another one writes what the last
		  still owed first, so a session that never gets to close cleanly
		  loses nothing
		- A .zdl now also carries the profile's name and whether it records
		  output or shares the port's settings, in a section of its own that
		  other Doom tools pass over.  [zdl.save] is unchanged
		- The interface is rewritten in Qt Quick.
		- Everything that used to open a dialog now happens over the window
		  instead
		- A file dialog now remembers where it last landed per kind of file,
		  and several external files can be added in one go
		- miniz is updated from 3.0.0 to 3.1.2, which fixes a central
		  directory offset overflow and an infinite loop in the inflater,
		  both on the path a PK3 is read through. It is now built without
		  its compressor and zlib shim, since ZDL only ever reads
	* Added:
		- Named launch profiles, selectable from the launch page
		- Files and list entries that have been moved or deleted are marked
		  as missing rather than failing at launch
		- Renaming an IWAD or a source port carries every profile that named
		  it along with it
		- Each profile can launch the source port on a config file of its own,
		  so its controls, video and sound settings are not shared with every
		  other profile.  The files are kept in $XDG_DATA_HOME/qzdl
		  (~/.local/share/qzdl) and %APPDATA%\qZDL, and a profile can be set
		  to bypass its own and use the port's instead
		- A source port can be marked as a DOS one, and is then launched
		  inside DOSBox rather than run as it is.  The flag is set per
		  port, either as it is added or when it is edited afterwards, and
		  is shown against the port wherever it is picked and on the cards
		  of the profiles that use it
		- Where DOSBox itself lives is set once in Settings.  Left empty,
		  whichever one the machine already has is used: the PATH is
		  searched, and on Windows what is installed under Program Files
		  as well
		- Launching one mounts every directory the launch names as a drive
		  of its own, the port's own being C:, and rewrites the paths it is
		  handed to match.  Switches no DOS port has ever taken are left
		  off, a command line longer than the 126 characters DOS allows is
		  passed in a response file instead, and a name DOSBox would
		  shorten to 8.3 is warned about before the port fails to open it
		- Nothing that cannot reach a DOS port is offered for one: the
		  multiplayer panel, the config file of its own, and the log of
		  what it printed are all left out while a profile is on one
		- The library opens on profiles or on games, whichever is set in
		  Settings
		- Settings can tell the user config to stand aside, so a config beside
		  the executable is opened instead.  An older ZDL could set that with
		  no way back
		- Build with -DSANITIZE=ON for the address and undefined sanitizers
	* Fixed:
		- The name an IWAD gives itself in its IWADINFO lump is now read.
		- A quoted argument no longer has every backslash in it doubled,
		  which reached the source port as a path only Windows' own
		  collapsing of repeated separators made work

